Package: remotedesktop-server
Version: @VERSION@
Section: net
Priority: optional
Architecture: @ARCHITECTURE@
Maintainer: Remote Desktop <noreply@example.invalid>
Homepage: https://github.com/gaozeqian/RemoteDesktop
Recommends: libmsquic
Description: Remote Desktop server (signaling, relay and ops console)
 Lets devices find each other and, when they cannot connect directly, forwards
 their traffic between them.
 .
 Three services are installed: the rendezvous server, which registers devices
 and hands out their codes; the relay, which carries the traffic of peers whose
 networks will not let them reach each other directly; and the ops console,
 which shows nodes, sessions, alerts and audit on 127.0.0.1:5090.
 .
 The two network servers are configured from /etc/remotedesktop-server, and the
 settings are kept there so that upgrading the package does not overwrite them.
 The console keeps its own database under /var/lib/remotedesktop-server and is
 loopback-only until a reverse proxy is put in front of it.
 .
 libmsquic is only recommended rather than required. It is what System.Net.Quic
 loads to speak QUIC at all, and without it every client fails with "QUIC is not
 supported on this system" — which reads like a platform problem rather than a
 missing package. It comes from Microsoft's own repository, so depending on it
 would make this package uninstallable on a machine that has not added that
 repository; the post-install step says so if it is missing.
